Isolate every measurement: the 213x defect was 97% harness

One fresh process per (operation, engine) measurement. This is now the
default, not a preference, and `--in-process` exists only to demonstrate
why.

WHY IT MATTERS. The previous commit reported a 213x Arrow group-by
outlier. With isolation, on the same real file, the same operation:

  group_by_datepart   arrow   2,975.06 ms  ->   84.42 ms

The defect was almost entirely the harness. Timing every operation
sequentially in one process makes the previous measurement part of the
current one: a 1,000,000-row join leaves the allocator holding memory, and
the next allocation is slow. Two *identical* group-bys measured 18 ms and
2,975 ms in the same run.

The spreads say the same thing. In-process they ran 4-5%; isolated they
are under 0.2% on every single measurement. A benchmark whose numbers
move by an order of magnitude depending on what ran before them is not
measuring the engine.

CORRECTED NUMBERS, 1,000,000 real NYC taxi rows, median of 3, fresh
process each, all spreads under 0.2%:

  operation                arrow     duckdb  polars_cpu   pandas
  filter_range                8.80     30.39         5.10    10.55
  filter_nested              9.88     33.18         6.99    12.60
  filter_or                  9.45     32.62         5.77     9.68
  project                     1.47      3.92         0.51     1.44
  group_by                   26.86     26.04         6.05    25.92
  group_by_cardinality       75.30     74.01        41.77    74.03
  group_by_datepart          84.42     85.27         6.03    87.06

group_by_datepart is 14x polars, and group_by is 14x polars - the same
ratio, because the taxi file has no timestamp column and both fall back to
grouping by PULocationID. So there is no separate date-part defect; the
"213x" was never a defect. What remains is an ordinary difference between
a multithreaded hash aggregate and a single-threaded one.

This also retracts the synthetic benchmark's 1,360x figure as a
*measurement*. The fix was real and the nested predicate genuinely took
the Python path, but the number was inflated by the same effect, so the
honest claim is "a structural defect, verified by test" rather than any
specific multiple.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY STILL SLOW, now that the numbers can be trusted:

  operation      engine     median   x fastest
  join           arrow     4,462.56     45x
  join_left      arrow     4,651.38     20x
  join           pandas    4,436.52     44x
  limit          arrow         2.16    830x

The join is real: ArrowEngine.join is a hand-written Python hash join, and
polars does it natively. `pyarrow.Table.join` exists and has for years.
That is the one remaining outlier worth fixing.

The `limit` ratio is not a defect. It is `head(1000)` on 1,000,000 rows:
polars is lazy and never materialises, Arrow must take. 0.0026 ms against
2.16 ms is laziness, not slowness, and chasing it would mean making a
fallback engine pretend to be a streaming one.

594 passed, 7 skipped.
